* Passive and discrete component models for OPA1641 mic simulation
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

* 1N4148 small signal diode
* Parameters from ON Semiconductor / Vishay published SPICE data
.model 1N4148 D (Is=2.52n N=1.752 Rs=0.568 Ikf=0 Xti=3 Eg=1.11
+ Cj0=4p M=0.4 Vj=0.75 Fc=0.5 Isr=1.48n Nr=2 Bv=100 Ibv=100u Tt=11.54n)

* BZX55C56 — 56V zener diode
* Vz=56V at Iz=5mA, Zz~70Ω
.model BZX55C56 D (Is=1n N=1 Rs=10 Cj0=50p Bv=56 Ibv=5m Vj=0.6 M=0.4)

* NTE10/3 transformer model (used reversed: 3-turn section as primary)
* The standard equivalent circuit of an audio transformer: winding resistance,
*   magnetising inductance with core loss Rc in parallel, and leakage (#132).
*   An ideal inductor without Rc rang with C_DC near 26 Hz (+1.4 dB at 30 Hz)
*   where the measurement has no such bump, and put the gain 2.4 to 4.3 dB
*   above the measurement; with Rc the shape matches and a flat 1.2 to 1.4 dB
*   remains, which the transformer cannot account for (a smaller Rc to cover it
*   spoils the low-end fit).
* Primary inductance (red-blue, 3-turn section): 10H, with Rc = 4k, fitted to a
*   configuration S sweep on the 3-turn wiring into 1.2 kohm (rms error 0.4 dB
*   from 20 to 300 Hz, against 1.1 dB for the best ideal inductor, 8 H). It is
*   consistent with Neutrik's spec of at most -2 dB from 20 Hz to 20 kHz in the
*   intended use (200 ohm source, 10 kohm load): if that -2 dB is at 20 Hz, the
*   1-turn side has about 0.7 H, i.e. about 6 H on the 3-turn section.
* Lp and Rc are fitted values; they trade against each other (14 H with 6k fits
*   nearly as well). Rc is taken as independent of frequency and level; real
*   core loss is neither. Measuring the transformer alone would pin them down.
* Secondary inductance (white-yellow, 1-turn): 10 / 9 = 1.111H  (Ls = Lp/n^2, n=3)
* Coupling k=0.9995: keeps the leakage inductance at (1 - k^2) x Lp = 10mH.
*   The measured sweep matches a model without leakage within 0.2dB to 15kHz.
* Primary DC resistance: 521Ω measured (Red-Blue, S1-S2); 540Ω on a second board
*   (red-black 1720Ω, blue-black 1182Ω: the tap sits at 0.314 of the winding)
* Secondary DC resistance: 42Ω measured (White-Yellow, P1-P2)
.subckt NTE10_3 PRI_A PRI_B SEC_P SEC_N
Rp1  PRI_A    PRI_A2   521
Lp   PRI_A2   PRI_B    10
Rc   PRI_A2   PRI_B    4k
Ls   SEC_P2   SEC_N    1.111
Rs1  SEC_P    SEC_P2   42
K1   Lp Ls    0.9995
.ends NTE10_3

* L78L24 simplified model: ideal 24V regulator with dropout
* Input on IN, output on OUT, ground on GND
* Quiescent current: ~3mA (modeled as current source from IN to GND)
.subckt L78L24 IN OUT GND_PIN
Vreg  OUT  GND_PIN  24
Rout  IN   INT_IN   0.1
Iq    INT_IN GND_PIN 3m
.ends L78L24

* BZX55C68 — 68V zener diode (DZ1 HV clamp, replaces BZX55C56)
.model BZX55C68 D (Is=1n N=1 Rs=10 Cj0=50p Bv=68 Ibv=5m Vj=0.6 M=0.4)

* BAT54S — Schottky diode (each junction modeled individually)
* V_F ~0.2V at low-µA loads; used in active Dickson pump
* Parameters from op-txm-v3 / Vishay datasheet
.model BAT54S D (IS=1e-6 N=1.05 BV=100 RS=3)
